   "kind": "theory-definition",
   "payloads": [
    {
     "op": "theory",
     "blockId": "p9-significant-figures-information",
     "replacement": "- **Significant figures** are the digits in a number that carry meaning.\n- A measurement is never exact. For example, you cannot cut timber to exactly $1.333\\,333\\ldots\\ \\mathrm{m}$, so digits beyond a certain figure are meaningless.\n- **Rules for significant figures:**\n  1. The first significant figure is the first non-zero digit, reading from left to right. Zeros before it are not significant.\n  2. All non-zero digits are significant.\n  3. Zeros between non-zero digits are significant.\n  4. Zeros after the last non-zero digit of a decimal are significant.\n  5. Zeros on the end of a whole number _may_ be significant.\n    Treat them as significant until we learn scientific notation.",
     "autoApply": true,
     "key": "theory|p9-significant-figures-information",
     "targetFingerprint": "0cae6072ecaacbd53cb2a5c689a1e05ad94714f6309c47899a9c20d92d026930"
    }
   ],
   "run": ".booklet-work/pedagogy-audit-v2/significant-figures-and-scientific-notation-v1/20261109-112304",
